cartridge "WIZARD OF IM/B.SPIRKO/1985"

' THE WIZARD OF IM
' A ColecoVision game based on "Wizard of Im" by Bob Spirko, Ahoy! July
' 1985 (Commodore 64 machine language at $C000, loaded and started with
' SYS 49152 by a two-line BASIC boot). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI
' made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' The game: glowing orbs fall down the night sky of Im. The wizard walks
' along the ground and fires magic sparks straight up to burst them. An
' orb that falls on the wizard costs a life (three lives). Orbs that reach
' the ground escape: from the second level each one costs points, and
' every tenth escaped orb costs a life. Points per orb rise with the
' level; the orbs fall faster and come more often.
'
' From the original (orig/wizard-of-im.dis, timed in jsc64 with
' orig/c64time-wizim.mjs): 19 lanes (the C64's even columns 0-36); one
' orb at most per row; every orb drops one row per "pass"; a pass lasts
' 21 x D loop rounds (D = 48 at level 1, 2 fewer each level: 17 frames a
' row at level 1); a new orb every 6 passes at level 1, one pass sooner at
' level 3 and then every 4 levels (never under 1); its lane is (RND & 15)
' + (RND & 3) (middle lanes come more often); an orb in the bottom row is
' removed at the end of the pass and costs level - 1 points when the
' score is 256 or more; one spark at a time, held fire fires again, the
' spark hits the orb in its lane; 5 points an orb at level 1, then the
' C64's table (8, 12, 19, 30, 48, 76, 121, 193, 30, 48, 76, 121, 193, 45,
' 72, 115, 184, 80, 105, 153); the next level at 768 points, then 1536,
' 2560, 3840... (3, 6, 10, 15, ... x 256); the wizard walks 1.85 C64
' pixels a frame, the spark rises 3.3; the wizard is struck by an orb
' that touches him: a flash, the orbs near the ground cleared, a life
' lost; game over after the third; the best score is kept.
'
' Liberties: the field is 19 tiles wide (the C64's 16-pixel lane pitch
' halved, speeds across scaled the same way) beside a side panel for the
' scores; the orbs glide down a pixel at a time (the C64 moved them a whole
' row at once, the rows one after another) and all orbs of a pass move
' together; collisions are box tests (the C64 used its sprite-to-text
' collision register); every tenth escaped orb costs a life (shown as ten
' rings in the panel): on the C64 orbs that got past cost nothing at level
' 1, so standing still under one lane with the fire button held was safe
' for ever; the levels stop rising at level 21 (the C64's fall delay would
' run out and its threshold byte wrap round), the score stops at 65535;
' no pause, restart or quit keys (joystick only); sounds for a hit, a
' miss, a new level and the start (the C64 had the spark and the struck
' wizard); GAME OVER then the title.
'
' Controls: controller 1 (or 2): the stick moves the wizard left and
' right, either button fires (hold it to fire again). On the title, up
' and down choose, a button chooses.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table; the title
' tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given.

